<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joey Smallwood</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Smallwood</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joey Smallwood was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> journalist and politician who led the campaign for confederation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in the referendums of 1948. After Newfoundland became Canada's tenth province in 1949, he served as its first premier until 1972, pursuing industrial schemes and the resettlement of outport communities. He is often called the last Father of Confederation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joey-smallwood.jpg" alt="Joey Smallwood" width="129" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Film Board of Canada. Photothèque / Library and Archives Canada / C-031327 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BradleyKingSmallwood1948_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Roberts Smallwood was born on 24 December 1900 at Mint Brook, near Gambo, and grew up in St. John's, where as a teenager he was apprenticed at a newspaper. In 1920 he moved to New York City and worked for the socialist paper The Call. Returning in 1925, he married Clara Oates and founded a newspaper in Corner Brook. He was a district campaign manager for Prime Minister Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-squires">Richard Squires</a> in 1928 and lost as a Liberal candidate in Bonavista in 1932. From 1937 his radio programme The Barrelman promoted pride in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>'s history and culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected in 1946 to the Newfoundland National Convention, Smallwood became the leading advocate of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, using the radio broadcasts of its proceedings to argue that it would bring prosperity, and he succeeded in putting the Canadian option on the ballot. He was a member of the 1947 Ottawa Delegation that negotiated the Terms of Union. His main opponents were Peter Cashin of the Responsible Government League and Chesley Crosbie of the Economic Union Party. In the second vote confederation carried by 77,869 to 71,464, or 52.3 percent; a strong rural vote and the promise of Canadian family allowances proved decisive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As Liberal leader Smallwood governed with large majorities, launching the Economic Development Plan of 1951 and relying heavily on German industrial expertise; his schemes had their best results in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydroelectricity">hydroelectricity</a>, iron mining and paper mills. A 1969 agreement bound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to sell electricity from Churchill Falls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> at a fixed rate until 2041. In 1959 he decertified the striking International Woodworkers of America and replaced it with a government-sponsored union, and critics often called him autocratic.</p><p>He announced his retirement in 1969 but then fought John Crosbie for the leadership. After the 1971 election gave the Liberals 20 seats to the Progressive Conservatives' 21, he resigned in January 1972 in favour of Frank Moores. In retirement he wrote the memoir I Chose Canada and began an Encyclopedia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a>, completed after his death. He was made a Companion of the Order of Canada in 1986 and died on 17 December 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joey Smallwood” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After two referendums in 1948, led by <b>Joey Smallwood's</b> campaign, it joined Canada as its tenth province on 31 March 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Dominion of Newfoundland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gambo, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-newfoundland-and-labrador">St. John's</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.78,-54.22&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Gambo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joey Smallwood"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5325 5939h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5325 5939h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.78,-54.22&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
